.\" nl.1 -- maux's nl(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An original page
.\" (this project's own text), so it carries no third-party notice
.\" -- MIT like the code, same posture as cut.1/fold.1. Rendered at
.\" build time to build/man/NL.TXT and staged on the play image as
.\" /usr/man/NL: `more /usr/man/NL`.
.TH NL 1 "October 8, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
nl \- line numbering filter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nl
[
.BI \-b " type"
] [
.BI \-f " type"
] [
.BI \-h " type"
] [
.BI \-d " delim"
] [
.BI \-i " incr"
] [
.BI \-n " format"
] [
.B \-p
] [
.BI \-s " sep"
] [
.BI \-v " start"
] [
.BI \-w " width"
] [
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Nl
copies
.I file
(standard input if none is given) to standard output, adding a
line number field to some or all lines.  At most one
.I file
is accepted.
.PP
The input is read as a sequence of
.I "logical pages,"
each with a header, a body, and a footer section.  A line that
consists of
.I exactly
the two\-character delimiter (backslash and colon, by default)
repeated three, two, or one time \(em
.BR \e:\e:\e: ,
.BR \e:\e: ,
or
.B \e:
\(em switches to the header, body, or footer section and is
itself consumed: it is never numbered or printed.  With no such
lines at all, the whole input is one page's body.
.PP
.BI \-b " type" ,
.BI \-h " type" ,
and
.BI \-f " type"
select which lines of the body (default
.BR t ),
header (default
.BR n ),
and footer (default
.BR n )
get a number:
.B a
numbers every line,
.B t
only non\-empty ones, and
.B n
none.
.BI p bre
numbers only the lines that contain a match for the basic regular
expression
.IR bre ,
which follows the
.B p
with no separator
.RB ( "\-b pfoo"
and
.B \-bpfoo
are the same).  The body, header, and footer each keep their own
expression.  An empty
.I bre
matches every line; a malformed one matches none.
.PP
.BI \-d " delim"
sets the delimiter to
.IR delim 's
one or two characters (a single character leaves the second at
its default colon).  By default, and unless
.B \-p
is given, encountering
.I any
delimiter line \(em header, body, or footer alike \(em resets
the line number to the
.B \-v
start value;
.B \-p
suppresses every one of those resets.
.PP
.BI \-n " format"
selects
.B ln
(left justified, no leading zeros),
.B rn
(right justified, no leading zeros \(em the default), or
.B rz
(right justified, zero\-padded).
.BI \-w " width"
sets the field width (default 6, capped at 20 in this
implementation).
.BI \-s " sep"
sets the text between the number field and the line (default one
tab); an unnumbered line still prints the field's own width in
blanks and the separator, just no digits.
.BI \-v " start"
sets the initial and reset line number (default 1);
.BI \-i " incr"
sets the increment (default 1, optionally signed).
.SH FILES
/usr/man/NL  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
cat(1), fold(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0; 1 if the file operand could not be opened; 2
for a usage error, including more than one file operand, a
malformed option value.
.SH BUGS
Line numbers are 16\-bit; the ceiling is 65535, and
.B \-v
/
.B \-i
arithmetic past it wraps silently.  A line longer than 200 bytes
is truncated at that length before being printed back.
